course: everyday
lesson: 512
title: The granny who spoils you and the strict grandad
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Burnley, Nan picks Alfie-Joe up from school every day while Mum's at work, and every day it's a Wagon Wheel, a can of pop and a fiver «for nowt» (Northern for nothing). Mum: «Mam, you spoil him rotten!» Nan: «That's what nans are for, our kid.» Alfie-Joe hides the fiver before Mum can argue. Lots of grandparents spoil their grandchildren: they often have more time and fewer rules than busy parents. In Brisbane, Nanna buys Ava-Rose an ice cream every arvo (Aussie for afternoon). Mum: «She'll rot her teeth!» Nanna: «She's a growing girl!»
explain: Then there's the strict grandad. In Carmarthen, Seren's grandad, who worked down the coal mine for thirty years, says, «Elbows off the table, sit up straight, no phones at the table.» «Mam lets me!» «This is my table, bach» (Welsh for little one). Seren rolls her eyes, then sees him wink. In Bristol, Nan says, «Children should be seen and not heard.» Delphine: «That's well old school, Nan.» Nan laughs: «It is, my lover. Now hush» («my lover» is just a friendly West Country «dear»). Strict grandparents grew up with different rules; their house, their rules.
explain: Strict and loving go together. In Philadelphia, Grandma makes Jaylon do homework before TV: «Back in my day, we did chores before we breathed.» «Grandma, chill.» He grumbles, and does it. In Aberdeen, Grandad takes Ewan fishing and says, «No talkin', ye'll scare the fish.» Three silent hours later Ewan lands his first fish and Grandad roars, «BRAW!» (Scots for great). In Leicester, Grandad bowls and Kamal bats in the back garden every afternoon: «Shabash!» (well done, in Urdu and Hindi).
explain: Some grandparents keep the family's traditions alive. In Los Angeles, Abuela (Spanish for grandma) lives with Mateo's family and cooks every day. His friends try her tamales and say they're «bussin'» (Gen Alpha slang for really tasty). Abuela doesn't know the word, but she laughs and gives them more. In Liverpool, Grandad does Tommy's school run in his ancient car, singing Beatles songs with the window down. Tommy's mates hear and laugh. Tommy turns red, then sings along louder.
explain: Different houses, different rules. In Manchester, Lyra's mum says bedtime is 8, but on Fridays at Gran's it's «Friday rules, love», and they watch telly till 10. In Kirkcaldy, Granny takes Islay to the chippy and whispers, «Dinnae tell yer mam we had chips again» (Scots for don't tell your mum). «Ma lips are sealed.» Small treat secrets like that are fine. A secret about safety, or one that makes you feel bad, never is. And don't play Nan against Mum: «Nan lets me!» starts rows. Both love you.
fact: Many grandparents spoil their grandchildren because they have more time and fewer rules than busy parents.
fact: Enjoying treats from grandparents is fine, but using them to argue with your parents causes rows.
fact: Strict grandparents grew up with different rules; at their house, their rules apply.
fact: Strict and loving can go together: rules and a wink, silence and a big cheer.
fact: Old sayings like «children should be seen and not heard» come from older times; you can respect them and smile at them.
fact: Grandparents can be coaches, teachers and friends.
fact: Grandparents who live with a family often keep its traditions, recipes and language alive.
fact: A small treat secret with a grandparent is fine; a secret about safety, or one that makes you feel bad, should always be told.
fact: Different houses can have different rules, like a later bedtime on Fridays at Gran's.
fact: Too many sweets and ice creams can rot your teeth, so treats are best now and then.
fact: «Nowt» means nothing; «our kid» is a Northern way to say a family member; «bach» is Welsh for little one; «arvo» is Aussie for afternoon; «braw» is Scots for great and «hen» is Scots for dear; «shabash» means well done; «bussin'» means really tasty.
exercise: What does Nan give Alfie-Joe «for nowt»? | A Wagon Wheel, pop and a fiver | A lecture about saving his pocket money | Nothing, she's strict | A list of chores to do before tea
exercise: What does «nowt» mean? | Nothing | Now | Lots | Next time
exercise: Why do many grandparents spoil their grandchildren? | More time, fewer rules | They're trying to make the parents look bad | They've been ordered to by the government | They want to be paid back when the child grows up
exercise: What was Seren's grandad's job? | Coal miner | Farmer | Teacher | Bus driver
exercise: What does Seren's grandad say when she says «Mam lets me»? | «This is my table, bach» | «Fine, have your phone, then» | «Then go and live with your Mam» | «Ask Mam, then»
exercise: What did Delphine say about «seen and not heard»? | «That's well old school, Nan» | «That's the best rule ever, Nan» | «Nobody's ever said that, Nan» | «I'll be silent for a week, Nan»
exercise: What does Grandma make Jaylon do before TV? | Homework | Cook dinner for the whole family | Walk the dog round the block | Nothing, he can watch TV right away
exercise: Why did Grandad say «No talkin'» on the fishing trip? | Talking would scare the fish | He was cross with Ewan that day | He had a sore throat | He was listening to the football
exercise: What does «braw» mean? | Great | Brave | Bad | Bored
exercise: What does Kamal's grandad shout when he hits the ball? | «Shabash!» (well done) | «Out!» (go and sit down) | «Hush!» (be quiet) | «Again!» (that was rubbish)
exercise: What did Mateo's mates mean when they said Abuela's tamales were «bussin'»? | Really tasty | Really cold | Really spicy and burnt | Really small portions
exercise: What did Tommy do when his mates laughed at Grandad's singing? | Turned red, then sang along | Told Grandad to stop singing forever | Pretended Grandad was a taxi driver | Walked to school on his own after that
exercise: What are «Friday rules» at Lyra's gran's? | Staying up watching telly till 10 | Bed at 8, same as home | No telly at all on Fridays | Homework before breakfast
exercise: Granny says, «Dinnae tell yer mam we had chips again». What is she asking? | Don't tell Mum about the chips | Do tell Mum about the chips tonight | Don't eat any more chips this week | Don't tell your friends about the chippy
exercise: What did Nanna say when Mum warned about Ava-Rose's teeth? | «She's a growing girl!» | «Teeth don't matter at all» | «Fine, no more ice cream ever» | «I'll pay the dentist»
exercise: When does Nanna buy Ava-Rose ice cream? | Every arvo, which is every afternoon | Every arvo, which is every morning | Every arvo, which is every evening | Only on birthdays and Christmas Day
test: Alfie-Joe gets a fiver every school day, Monday to Friday. How much does he get in 2 weeks? | £50 | £25 | £10 | £70
test: Lyra's bedtime at home is 8; at Gran's on Fridays it's 10. How much later is that? | 2 hours | 10 hours | 18 hours | 1 hour
test: Alfie-Joe, at home: «Nan lets me have pop AND a fiver, so you have to as well, Mum». What's likely? | A row, and Mum won't like being played against Nan | Mum will say yes and give him a tenner | Nan will be in trouble with the police | Nothing, Mum will just agree with Nan
test: Granny: «Don't tell yer mam about the chips, hen». A man at the park says: «Don't tell your mum I gave you sweets». Which secret must be told? | The man's | Granny's | Both should be kept | Neither should be told
test: Seren: «Grandad's well strict, he must hate me». What's truer? | Strict and loving go together | Strict grandads never love anyone | He only likes Mam, not her | Strict means he wants her gone
test: Ewan, after 3 hours of silence: «that was proper boring, Grandad». Grandad: «Aye, but ye caught a fish, didn't ye?» What's the point? | Quiet times can end in something great | Fishing is always boring and pointless | Talking scares away all the fish forever | Grandads never let you talk at all
test: In a cartoon, Grandma is a sweet old lady who only knits and naps. What's truer? | Grandparents can be coaches, cooks and friends | Grandparents only ever knit and nap | Grandparents can't play any sports | All grannies are spoiling and never strict
test: Tommy's mate: «your grandad's singin' old songs in that rust bucket, cringe». What did Tommy do that we can learn from? | Joined in and stopped being embarrassed | Told his grandad to leave him at the gates | Laughed at his own grandad with them | Never let his grandad drive him again
test: What would most likely happen if Nanna bought Ava-Rose two ice creams every arvo for a year? | Her teeth could be harmed | Her teeth would get stronger | Nothing at all would happen | She'd grow twice as fast
test: Mateo's friend: «your abuela's tamales are bussin', no cap». Abuela doesn't know the word. Why does she laugh and give them more? | She can tell they love her food | She thinks they're being rude | She wants them to go home | She doesn't care what they say
test: «Back in my day we did chores before we breathed!» What does Grandma really mean? | Children used to do lots of chores early | Children used to hold their breath | Children never did any chores | She was born without breathing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the tendency of grandparents to indulge their grandchildren? | It commonly arises from greater leisure and fewer rules | It is attributable to a wish to embarrass the parents | It is required of grandparents by statute | It indicates an absence of affection for the parents
exam: Identify the rule which ought to govern a child's conduct at the residence of a strict grandparent. | The rules of that house apply | The child's rules from home apply without exception | No rules apply at a grandparent's residence | The child may select whichever rule is most convenient
exam: Ascertain which category of secret, among the following, must invariably be disclosed to a parent. | A secret concerning safety, or one that causes the child distress | A secret concerning the consumption of chips at a chip shop | A secret concerning a surprise prepared for a birthday | A secret concerning an additional hour of television viewing
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the invocation of a grandparent's permissions in argument with a parent. | It tends to provoke disputes | It invariably secures the parent's consent | It is recommended as a means of negotiation | It is without any consequence whatsoever
